  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/017b40453041b9485436e21f4966e530b925f6989795283e664692265ca2f814.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{M}_{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{14}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=3$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3\\cdot {M}_{11}+3\\cdot {M}_{12}+3\\cdot {M}_{13}+3\\cdot {M}_{14} \\\\  & =3({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}+{M}_{14}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6ceecc26bb9800a2884202b05144dcecbf988bea7953605e4b33e485a4cb1865.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/f9ec123fe8048f8463c845527162a3f28ecc3a597f7f57717dde88909f363906.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,0)=20 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,1)=17 \\\\ {M}_{13} & =f(2,2)=14 \\\\ {M}_{14} & =f(2,3)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3(20+17+14+11) \\\\  & =186.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
